 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Sydney rents are still rising, but much more slowly than at the peak.\n\n- **Latest:** Sydney rents were up **3.5% year-on-year in August 2026**, the most recent month in the CPI data.\n- **Peak:** Annual growth hit **8.8% in March 2024**, the highest in this series, and stayed above 8% for much of 2023–24.\n- **Recent trend:** Growth fell through 2025, bottomed at 3.2% in August and September 2025, and has held between 3.3% and 3.5% through 2026.\n- **Monthly pace:** Rents rose 0.2–0.4% each month in 2026, about 3.5% annualised.\n- **National comparison:** Australia-wide rents were up 3.6% year-on-year in August 2026, so Sydney is in line with the national figure after running hotter than it for most of 2023–24.\n\nSo the acute squeeze has eased. Rents are rising at a moderate pace, not the double-digit-style jumps of 2023. The CPI rent measure covers all rents, including tenants who have stayed put, so it lags what new tenants face when they sign a lease. I didn't pull vacancy rates or wage growth, so I can't say whether rents are rising faster than incomes.",
